A||N||12||87ECO5||High mileage cars - what to watch out for?||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||10-18-2000||07:26 AM||||Other than the engine and tranny, what else should someone watch out for when buying a car with high miles - say 200,000 miles?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||10-18-2000||07:39 AM||||Rust, ball joints, worn bushings, hoses, wheel bearings. Can't think of anything else right now.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||10-18-2000||08:02 AM||||I knew this was coming. I even told William last night what I thought you would be doing soon. You're getting rid of the coupe and buying a 200,000 mile Civic, right? Tell me I'm wrong. Maybe it's just a 200,000 mile Fiero.<BR>I'd look for what Phil says. Also, check any frame parts for possible accident damage, and run a carfax report.||||reg||1
Z||000003||87ECO5||10-18-2000||08:05 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.classifieds2000.com/cgi-cls/ad.exe?partner=c2k&path=Auto~Car~Details&recordid=1045244&hasphoto=0&keywordqueryid=258295498&titlesummary=title" TARGET=_blank>http://www.classifieds2000.com/cgi-cls/ad.exe?partner=c2k&path=Auto~Car~Details&recordid=1045244&hasphoto=0&keywordqueryid=258295498&titlesummary=title</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||10-18-2000||08:23 AM||||Holy Cow! You're thinking about buying another Fiero! I was just thinking how unenthused you seemed to the whole Fiero bit, I thought you were getting out.<BR>Glad to see that is not the case. It's not a bad deal, but look at the distance you would have to cover to retreive it. There is a problem though. It's a fastback, and you know what I think about fastback Fieros, maybe you should convert it over to notchback. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||10-18-2000||08:42 AM||||Wow, Scott!  I'm with Drew, though - that sure is a long way to go to get a $1000 car.  Wonder what that interior looks like after 200K miles...paint is probably nothing to write home about either.  Still, $1000 for a strong-running, complete GT is a great deal.<P>Did he have the clutch done when the engine was rebuilt?||||reg||1
Z||000006||87ECO5||10-18-2000||09:17 AM||||Don't get excited.  I'm not really interested.  Just wondering ...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||10-18-2000||11:29 AM||||Brakes, suspension components, steering rack, exhaust manifold, exhaust system, catalytic converter, radiator, a/c, electric components, heater core, heater fan, seats bolted to the floor(?), cracked dash, center consul warping, ecm, fuel tank straps, coolent lines, coolent line brackets, fuel pump, fuel lines, brake lines, head light motors||||reg||3
Z||000008||Carrolles||10-18-2000||12:15 PM||||Old Lar I believe you forgot to mention Johnson Rod.  Otherwise I think you covered just about everything.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Old Lar||10-18-2000||01:49 PM||||I forgot the muffler barings.  It's just that a 200000 mile Fiero is pretty wore out and unless it was highly maintained these are all the things that can go wrong.  My 88 with 54000 miles on it had most of those things wrong.  Except for no rust(a Florida car).  I know people who have 200000+ miles on their Fieros and they are still running strong.  But if I was looking at a 200000+ Fiero, I'm expecting lots may be wrong with it.||||reg||10
Z||000010||Tigger||10-18-2000||02:43 PM||||I know this is going to sound crazy...<P>Check and tighten the starter bolts.      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggertoon.gif">               <BR>      <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A>  <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-18-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000011||Pontiaddict||10-18-2000||08:21 PM||||Make sure the rear toe links are securely affixed to the engine cradle. My 84 had a nice tire wobble at about 60.||||reg||1
Z||000012||mshill||10-18-2000||09:14 PM||||You can bet that you will likely need to replace the entire cooling system when you get it as well.  My 86SE had 185K on it and <BR>in addition to doing the suspension, I replaced radiator, hoses, heater core, etc. I bought it for $750 and put another $2500 into it (parts only) and that includes an engine overhaul, clutch, water pump, cat, and poly bushings all around.<P>When I tore the engine apart it was immaculate inside.  I have no doubt the previous owner changed the oil religiously.<BR>||||reg||1
